<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="loading-plugins" title="Loading and isolating plugins">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>
            No shipped driver loads a third-party <code>WeaverPlugin</code> on its own — see
            <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a>.
        </p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        Two routes put a <code>WeaverPlugin</code> in front of a weaver. Both belong to the program
        that builds one. Neither puts your plugin behind a class loader, reduced JVM privileges, or
        a clock.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Your program builds a weaver"] --> B["plugin(instance)"]
    A --> C["discoverPlugins(loader)"]
    C --> D["ServiceLoader lists the providers"]
    D -->|"AW3114"| E["Nothing from that loader"]
    D --> F["Each constructor called on its own"]
    B --> G["Candidates"]
    F --> G
    G --> H["Sorted by namespace, then class"]
    H --> I["Four gates, one plugin at a time"]
    I --> J["contribute, inside the guard"]
    J --> K["Registry frozen"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="The two routes in" id="routes">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="An instance you hand over">
                <p>
                    <code>Weaver.builder().plugin(p)</code> adds <code>p</code> to the candidates.
                    The order you add in does not survive. Every gate applies to it, exactly as to
                    a discovered plugin.
                    <a href="plugin-lifecycle.topic">The plugin lifecycle</a> has the four.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A class loader you name">
                <p>
                    <code>discoverPlugins(loader)</code> asks <code>ServiceLoader</code> for every
                    <code>WeaverPlugin</code> on that loader. Discovery runs only when a loader was
                    set. What it finds is appended to the instances you registered.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Neither">
                <p>
                    The weaver still gets the engine's own built-in plugin. The builder installs
                    that one directly. Every shipped driver is in this case, and
                    <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> compares the four.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <warning>
            <p>
                One unreadable service declaration costs every plugin on that loader. A throw while
                <code>ServiceLoader</code> lists the providers is reported as <code>AW3114</code>,
                and the loader hands back an empty list. Check every
                <code>META-INF/services</code> entry for <code>WeaverPlugin</code>. Each line must
                name a public class with a public no-argument constructor. A constructor that
                throws costs only its own plugin.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Which namespaces may load" id="permission">
        <p>
            One predicate over <code>PluginId</code>, passed to <code>permitPlugins(...)</code>,
            decides whether a namespace may load.
            <a href="plugin-lifecycle.topic">The plugin lifecycle</a> has the gate and its
            diagnostic. The built-in plugin skips it and one other of the four gates. No
            predicate can leave a weaver without injectors.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>aether.weaver.plugins.enabled</code>, <code>.allow</code> and <code>.deny</code>
            are names no driver reads. <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a>
            has the family, and <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a> how they would weigh
            against each other.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What the guard contains" id="contains">
        <p>
            Every call into plugin code crosses one boundary. It catches <code>Throwable</code>
            and reports the phase's diagnostic. Most phases name the plugin; two name the point's
            identifier or the contributed kind instead.
            <a href="reporting.topic">Report rather than throw</a> has what each covered call
            costs, and which calls it does not cover.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <code>VirtualMachineError</code> is re-thrown untouched. What happens next is the
            driver's. The agent halts by default, or continues under
            <code>aether.weaver.onError=report</code>; the class loader driver does not catch it
            at all. A <code>LinkageError</code> is contained instead, and its remedy names the SPI
            generation rather than the phase — <a href="spi.topic#values">The plugin SPI</a> has the
            level to return.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What isolation does not promise" id="limits">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="No class loader of its own">
                <p>
                    The engine creates none. Your plugin's classes come from the loader you named,
                    or from the instance you passed. They sit beside the engine's.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="No reduced privileges">
                <p>
                    A plugin reads the files, sockets and system properties the build process can.
                    Review a jar before you permit it. The predicate is where you say no.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="No time or memory limit">
                <p>
                    Nothing interrupts a plugin, and nothing caps what it allocates. A
                    <code>contribute</code> that blocks blocks the thread building the weaver.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="No separation of state">
                <p>
                    Every call runs on the caller's thread. For <code>ClassWoven</code> that is the
                    weaving thread. Nothing keeps a plugin away from what the rest of the classpath
                    holds. Report through the context rather than throwing, which
                    <a href="reporting.topic">Report rather than throw</a> covers.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            Sorting is one of several places plugin code runs outside the guard, not the only one.
            <a href="plugin-lifecycle.topic">The plugin lifecycle</a> has the sort itself: a throw
            from <code>id()</code> there ends the build with no diagnostic. Return a constant from
            <code>id()</code>, which <a href="write-a-plugin.topic">Write a plugin</a> shows.
        </p>
    </chapter>

